concept primitives {
  name     = "Primitive Shapes"
  summary  = "The page primitives everything lowers to: rect, circle, line, label, polygon, container, image, card, node_table."
  related  = [formatting, columns]
  audience = :both
  body {
    p <<PROSE
Primitive shapes are what everything is broken down into for rendering. Higher-level blocks (flowchart nodes, charts, cards, …) all lower to these, so targeting a new backend only means re-implementing the primitives. They live inside a `diagram { … }`.
PROSE

    h2 "Shared fields"
    table {
      header = ["Field", "Meaning"]
      rows = [
        ["`id`", "Name used to connect the shape (`a -> b`) and to anchor others to it."],
        ["`class`", "Style classes — text and SVG paint (`fill`, `stroke`, …) via the class system."],
        ["`anchor_left` / `anchor_right` / `anchor_top` / `anchor_bottom`", "Fractional anchors (0–1) that pin an edge of the shape to the parent box."],
        ["`connect_points`", "Which sides (`:left`/`:right`/`:top`/`:bottom`) edges attach to."],
        ["`link`", "Make the shape a hyperlink to an in-site page (bare page name, or `site:page`)."],
      ]
    }
    p <<PROSE
The box-like shapes — `rect`, `circle`, and `container` — additionally accept an icon badge: `icon` (a `pack.name`), `icon_size`, `icon_pos`, and `icon_class`. Any shape with a `link` is wrapped in a clickable `<a>`.
PROSE

    h2 "rect, circle, line"
    code "wcl" { source = <<'SRC'
diagram { width = 200  height = 90
  rect { x = 20.0  y = 15.0  width = 120.0  height = 60.0  fill = "#cce"  stroke = "#333" }
}
diagram { width = 200  height = 100
  circle { cx = 100.0  cy = 50.0  r = 36.0  fill = "#8fbcbb"  stroke = "#333" }
}
diagram { width = 200  height = 80
  line { x1 = 20.0  y1 = 60.0  x2 = 180.0  y2 = 20.0  stroke = "#bf616a" }
}
SRC
    }

    h2 "label and polygon"
    p <<PROSE
`label` is an SVG text label — named `label` (not `text`) to avoid clashing with the paragraph block. `polygon` is an arbitrary closed shape from a list of points.
PROSE
    code "wcl" { source = <<'SRC'
diagram { width = 200  height = 60
  label "Hello" { x = 100.0  y = 36.0  font_size = 22.0  fill = "#5e81ac" }
}
diagram { width = 200  height = 100
  polygon { points = "40,80 100,15 160,80"  fill = "#ebcb8b"  stroke = "#333" }
}
SRC
    }

    h2 "container"
    p <<PROSE
A grouping box that holds and lays out child shapes (`@children`). Optional chrome makes the group visible; a `layout` arranges the children automatically.
PROSE
    code "wcl" { source = <<'SRC'
diagram { width = 240  height = 130
  container {
    anchor_left = 10.0  anchor_top = 10.0
    fill = "#eef"  stroke = "#88a"  padding = 10.0
    layout = :grid  columns = 2  cell_width = 90.0  cell_height = 44.0  gap = 10.0
    rect { fill = "#88c0d0" }
    rect { fill = "#a3be8c" }
    rect { fill = "#ebcb8b" }
    rect { fill = "#b48ead" }
  }
}
SRC
    }

    h2 "image and card"
    p <<PROSE
`image` is a raster image placed as an SVG `<image>` — its source is a doc-relative path (copied into `_wdoc/`) or a URL / `data:` URI. `card`'s `body` is arbitrary wdoc content (paragraphs, callouts, lists, even nested diagrams), wrapped in an SVG `<foreignObject>` so it scales with the diagram.
PROSE
    code "wcl" { source = <<'SRC'
diagram { width = 260  height = 110
  card { x = 20.0  y = 15.0  width = 220.0  height = 80.0
    title = "Note"
    p "Rich **text** inside a diagram."
  }
}
SRC
    }

    h2 "node_table"
    p <<PROSE
A titled box built from a stack of **rows**, each holding arbitrary wdoc content (like a `card`) and exposing its own **connection point** — so an edge attaches to a single row (a foreign-key column, a class field) rather than the whole box. Ideal for database / ER tables and UML class diagrams. An edge targets a row by that row's `id`.
PROSE
    code "wcl" { source = <<'SRC'
diagram { width = 420  height = 170  routing = :straight
  node_table { id = users
    x = 20.0  y = 20.0  width = 150.0
    title = "users"
    node_row { id = users_id    p "id: int" }
    node_row { id = users_email p "email: text" }
  }
  node_table { id = orders
    x = 250.0  y = 20.0  width = 150.0
    title = "orders"
    node_row { id = orders_id      p "id: int" }
    node_row { id = orders_user_id p "user_id: int" }
  }
  orders_user_id -> users_id :data
}
SRC
    }
  }
}
